# p5/contract/paths -- THE ON-DISK CONTRACT: every path P5 owns.
#
# This is the DESIGN-TIME inventory, and it is the SINGLE SOURCE from which the
# shipped code derives. It bounds what P5 may put on a box; it is read at run
# time by p5-common.sh, and everything else is derived from it:
#
#   what the installer may place   p5_check_dest consults it LAST, so a path
#                                  inside the namespace that nobody declared
#                                  here is refused (exit 4).
#   what the installer records     the installer's own file set is checked
#                                  against the `install` rows below and ABORTS
#                                  on divergence in either direction.
#   which directories are recorded a created directory enters installed.dirs
#                                  only if it is a `dir` row here. Shared system
#                                  directories the install had to create
#                                  (/usr/sbin, /etc/init.d) are NOT rows here,
#                                  so they can never be recorded and E7 can
#                                  never try to remove them.
#   what "clean" means             the P5-half clean predicate probes EVERY
#                                  non-staging row below. Adding a row extends
#                                  the predicate automatically.
#   what --remove removes          the removal plan is the install-time record
#                                  RE-VALIDATED against this file, plus the
#                                  post-install states below removed by the
#                                  mechanism that created them.
#
# Before this was derived, the removal list and the clean predicate were two
# hand-written lists. Both were wrong in the same way and neither could see the
# other: a clean install placed 9 files and recorded 6, and a root carrying
# seven declared P5 paths reported CLEAN. Deriving both from this file is the
# fix; patching the two lists would have left the defect class alive.
#
# WHAT REMAINS HAND-MAINTAINED, stated so nobody has to go looking:
#   1. This file. A human adds a row. Bars NS-2/NS-3/NS-4/NS-5 constrain its
#      shape, its disjointness from contract/foreign and its agreement with
#      contract/namespace, but nothing can invent the row itself.
#   2. p5-install's source->destination->mode map for the product's OWN files.
#      The contract is about destinations; it cannot carry "copied from where,
#      with what mode". That map plus the installer's list of files it
#      SYNTHESISES (the stamp and the two records) must together equal exactly
#      the `install`-state file rows below -- p5-install asserts set equality in
#      BOTH directions and aborts on any divergence (bar IN-14/IN-15).
#   3. contract/namespace's patterns. NS-4 asserts every row here is admitted by
#      one, so the two cannot drift into different spellings of the same claim.
# Nothing else is a list. In particular there is no hand-written removal list
# and no hand-written clean predicate any more.
#
# It is still NOT what E7 removes VERBATIM. Removal is driven by
# /usr/lib/p5/installed.files + installed.dirs, the INSTALL-TIME record of what
# this particular install actually placed, RE-VALIDATED row by row against this
# file before anything is unlinked. Two levels on purpose --
#
#   design-time (this file)   what P5 is ALLOWED to own. A path absent here is
#                             not P5's, so removal must not touch it, and the
#                             installer refuses to write it (contract/namespace
#                             is the coarse machine-checked form of the same
#                             rule; this file is the exact one).
#   install-time (the record) what P5 DID own on THIS box at THIS version.
#                             Exact, per-file sha256, survives version skew.
#
# The re-validation is what makes version skew safe. A record written by an
# older, buggier installer can name a path this file does not declare; the
# remover REFUSES THE WHOLE REMOVAL and names the offending rows rather than
# trusting the record. That is the mechanism that makes "rm -rf a directory P5
# does not exclusively own" impossible rather than unlikely.
#
# Anything E7 removes that is NOT in the install-time record is old-stack
# removal, which is a different list with a different owner (U26) and a
# different justification (contract/foreign enumerates it).
#
# FORMAT: kind|role|path|owner|state|note
#   kind   dir | file | glob | staging | uci
#   role   client | server | both
#   owner  the epic/unit that puts it there (E0 = this unit)
#   state  WHO creates it and WHEN. This is what decides whether the
#          install-time record can possibly cover it:
#          install   placed by p5-install itself, every install, unconditional,
#                    and RECORDED
#          payload   placed by p5-install FROM THE PACKAGE FILEMAP (U28 builds
#                    the filemap; the file exists only if the package ships it)
#          reserved  declared so removal's scope is known, NOTHING writes it yet
#          transient written by p5-install or p5-uninstall DURING a run and
#                    removed before it exits. Never recorded -- its PRESENCE is
#                    the signal that a run died half way (see below)
#          runtime   created by P5 while running, not by the installer
#          enable    created AFTER install, by procd, when a service is enabled
#          uci       a named UCI object, not a filesystem path
#   note   why it exists / what it holds
#
# `reserved` rows are the point of this file: removal is being written before
# E5/E6 have shipped their files, so it needs the SHAPE of the final install,
# not just today's. A reserved row is a claim on a path, not a promise that a
# file appears there. Its owner unit either fills it or deletes the row.
#
# THE STATES THAT THE INSTALL-TIME RECORD CANNOT COVER, and why they exist.
# The record is written by the installer, so it is structurally incapable of
# containing anything created after the installer exits. Four classes:
#   transient /usr/lib/p5/{install,remove}.inprogress -- the INTENT records.
#            Written and synced BEFORE the first destructive step, unlinked
#            after the last. A completion stamp written afterwards cannot do
#            this job: between the first write and the stamp there is a window
#            in which the box carries P5 files and no record of them, and that
#            window is exactly where "wedged against its own reinstall" lives.
#   runtime  /var/run/p5 (tmpfs, cleared by reboot) and /etc/p5/* (E6's facts,
#            written on the box by E6's fact-writer, never by the installer --
#            an installer that seeds configuration is an installer that ships a
#            constant).
#   enable   /etc/rc.d/[SK]??p5-* -- `/etc/init.d/<svc> enable` writes these
#            symlinks. The deployed stack already treats that flag as lifecycle
#            STATE in writing (p2-engarde/bondctl:15 "State owners: engagement
#            = rc.d flag", p1-autorate/autoratectl:11, 98-wg-autorate:9,
#            99-tether-autorate:49). E5 ships /etc/init.d/p5-datapath and
#            /etc/init.d/p5-server, so P5 WILL create them.
#   uci      a named object inside a config file P5 does not own.
# Removal must not look for these in installed.files. It removes them by the
# mechanism that created them -- `disable` for rc.d, `uci delete` for a uci
# object, a bounded sweep of the declared glob for anything left over -- and
# the P5-half clean predicate probes them all, so a leftover is visible.

# ---- product metadata root (the thing that makes a box interrogable) --------
dir |both  |/usr/lib/p5                     |E0 |install |product metadata root
file|both  |/usr/lib/p5/stamp               |E0 |install |version + provenance stamp; what `p5-version` prints
file|both  |/usr/lib/p5/installed.files     |E0 |install |sha256sum-format record of every file this install placed, ITSELF INCLUDED (one self-referential row)
file|both  |/usr/lib/p5/installed.dirs      |E0 |install |directories this install CREATED and OWNS, and which are declared `dir` rows here (so removal rmdirs only those)
file|both  |/usr/lib/p5/p5-common.sh        |E0 |install |shared sh library sourced by the on-box entry points
file|both  |/usr/lib/p5/contract-namespace  |E0 |install |the shipped copy of contract/namespace, so an installed box can re-check its own scope offline
file|both  |/usr/lib/p5/contract-foreign    |E0 |install |the shipped copy of contract/foreign, for the same reason
file|both  |/usr/lib/p5/contract-paths      |E0 |install |the shipped copy of THIS file. The on-box entry points derive the clean predicate and the removal plan from it, and it is what a stale record is re-validated against

# ---- the intent records: how a half-finished run stays finishable ----------
# Written before the first destructive step of the run they name, removed after
# the last. They are NOT in installed.files (they are gone by the time it is
# written) and they are NOT the stamp (the stamp says a run FINISHED; these say
# a run STARTED). Their presence is a box state the tools name and act on.
file|both  |/usr/lib/p5/install.inprogress  |E0 |transient|the install plan, synced before the first file is placed. Present => an install died half way; the plan it carries is what --remove undoes
file|both  |/usr/lib/p5/remove.inprogress   |E0 |transient|the removal plan, synced before the first unlink. Present => a removal died half way; re-running --remove resumes from it

# ---- on-box entry points ---------------------------------------------------
# p5-install is NOT installed: it belongs to the package, not to the box. A box
# with no package cannot install; a box with no package MUST still be able to
# describe, protect and remove itself, which is why the other three ARE
# installed.
file|both  |/usr/sbin/p5-uninstall          |E0 |install |single uninstall entry point. The P5 half (--check/--list/--remove) is E0's and is implemented. The old-stack half is E7's and is no longer one verb: --quiescent is the PRE-SWITCH gate (U208, implemented), --purge the composite reset (implemented, client-only), and --switch-off/--remove-old the two halves of the client order Mo settled on 2026-09-03 (U209, IN FLIGHT as of 2026-09-05). Not --check --scope both: presence is the wrong question before the switch (U214)
file|both  |/usr/sbin/p5-version            |E0 |install |provenance/interrogation entry point
file|both  |/usr/sbin/p5-deadman            |E0 |install |the automatic-rollback primitive any step that can cut the management path must arm before it runs. Deadline-based, so the timer, the boot check and a manual fire are ONE code path

# ---- configuration and runtime roots ---------------------------------------
dir |both  |/etc/p5                         |E0 |install |P5 fact/config root; E6's fact-writer populates it
dir |both  |/etc/p5/deadman                 |E0 |install |armed deadman records. On persistent storage on purpose: a deadman that only exists in a running process does not survive the power loss it is there to protect against
glob|both  |/etc/p5/deadman/*               |E0 |runtime |one file per armed deadman: deadline, restore command, label. Created by `p5-deadman arm`, removed by `confirm` or by firing. Never installed
dir |both  |/var/run/p5                     |E0 |runtime |tmpfs runtime state; created by P5 while running, so it can NEVER be in installed.dirs. Removal clears it by the declared literal path from THIS file, never from the record, and a reboot clears it anyway

# ---- the transport secret: made on the box, never shipped (U115) -----------
# The bonded frame's MAC is keyed with ONE 32-byte secret that must be the same
# 32 bytes at each end (p4-bondagg/daemon/auth.go:117-120, and the server's
# mirror at p4-bondagg/server/auth.go:196-204). When the file is absent
# LoadKeys fails, the failure is logged, and the daemon keeps running with
# authentication OFF -- byte-for-byte the pre-U31 wire. So the file's ABSENCE
# is a security state, not a missing nicety, and that is why it gets a row.
#
# WHY THIS ROW IS ADMISSIBLE WHERE A FILEMAP ROW UNDER /etc/p5 IS NOT.
# IN-16 is enforced by p5_check_dest, which admits only states install,
# payload and reserved (p5/lib/p5-common.sh:673). The /etc/p5/* row further
# down is state=runtime, so a package that ships a fact file into /etc/p5 is
# still refused with exit 4 -- with this row present exactly as without it,
# because this row covers one literal path and not the fact set. What IN-16
# exists to stop is an installer SHIPPING A CONSTANT NOBODY MEASURED: the
# /etc/bond/agg_w 20000,15000 defect, removed in 52a76d3. A secret has no
# constant to ship. Nothing is carried in the package; the bytes are read from
# /dev/urandom ON THE BOX at install time, or adopted from a file the operator
# names so that the two boxes can carry one secret. The line the contract
# draws is therefore not "which directory" but "did the installer invent a
# value it could not measure", and this side of it is admitted.
#
# state=install, not runtime, and that is the load-bearing choice: the
# installer does place it on every install, so installed.files must record it
# and removal must take it away. A secret that outlives the product it
# authenticated is the failure mode this row's state prevents.
file|both  |/etc/p5/transport.key           |E0 |install |the per-install transport secret the frame MAC is keyed with: 64 hex characters on one line, mode 600. SYNTHESISED on the box by p5-install from /dev/urandom, or adopted from a file the operator names with --transport-key. Never carried in the package and never printed. Recorded in installed.files like every other placed file, so removal takes it with the rest

# ---- payload: shipped by the package, placed from the filemap --------------
file|client|/usr/sbin/p5-datapath           |E2 |reserved|the pull-core client daemon (U7). Built as p4-bondagg/daemon -> CI artifact bond-agg-linux-<arch>; installs under a p5-* name because /usr/sbin/bond-agg is on the OLD stack's removal list (contract/foreign)
file|server|/usr/sbin/p5-server             |E3 |reserved|the thin server daemon (U16). Built as p4-bondagg/server -> CI artifact bondsrv-linux-arm64
file|both  |/usr/sbin/p5                    |E5 |reserved|the operator CLI. Name and verbs are E5's; E0 only reserves the path
file|client|/etc/init.d/p5-datapath         |E5 |reserved|procd service for the client daemon. START/STOP priorities are E5's to derive -- E0 ships no init script and invents no priority
file|server|/etc/init.d/p5-server           |E5 |reserved|procd service for the server daemon
glob|client|/etc/hotplug.d/iface/[0-9][0-9]-p5 |E5 |reserved|netifd hook. kind=glob because the two-digit priority is deliberately UNSET here -- choosing it is choosing an ordering against netifd's other hooks, which is E5's derivation to make and justify. See contract/namespace note 3
file|both  |/etc/config/p5                  |E5 |reserved|UCI config, IF E5 chooses UCI over plain files in /etc/p5. If it does not, delete this row and the matching namespace pattern rather than leaving an unused claim

# ---- reconciler/ecod/watchdog + the DAG table + their runtime glob (ADR-005 §5, U51) ----
# E0's own contract used to mark these paths FOREIGN (contract/foreign, pre-U51)
# while deploy/p5 shipped them under bond-* names -- ADR-005 resolves the
# contradiction by moving the destinations into the p5-* namespace, not by
# widening namespace to admit bond-*. These rows are the destination map
# ADR-005 §4/§5 derived; U51a executes the rename in the shipped files.
file|client|/usr/sbin/p5-reconciler        |E5 |reserved|the level-triggered reconciler (today deploy/p5/bond-xctl); operator-facing verbs are reconcile[/refresh], node, selfcheck -- internal diagnostic sub-verbs not enumerated here (L-4: no verb name in this row)
file|client|/usr/sbin/p5-ecod              |E5 |reserved|connectivity/failover daemon (today deploy/p5/bond-ecod). MAY BE DELETED by U50b if the pull datapath covers eco/lightning -- delete the row rather than leave an unused claim
file|client|/usr/sbin/p5-watchdog          |E5 |reserved|supervision + passive tput sensor (today deploy/p5/bond-watchdog)
file|client|/etc/init.d/p5-ecod            |E5 |reserved|procd service for p5-ecod. Priorities are E5's derivation; E0 invents none
file|client|/etc/init.d/p5-watchdog        |E5 |reserved|procd service for p5-watchdog. Priorities are E5's derivation
file|client|/usr/lib/p5/dag                |E5 |reserved|the DAG table (today deploy/p5/bond.dag). Product data, NOT configuration: it is package-shipped, read-only and version-locked, and /etc/p5 is barred to the installer by IN-16. role narrowed from ADR-005's draft `both` to `client` -- the server does not run a DAG today (ADR-005 §5 correction)
# U124: the reconciler is an ENTRY POINT plus five sourced libraries. Same class
# as the dag row above -- package-shipped product data, read-only, version-locked
# with the bin -- so the same kind, role, owner and state. Five rows and not one
# glob: p5_check_dest consults this file to decide whether a destination is
# declared, and a glob would declare `/usr/lib/p5/xctl-anything.sh` as installable.
# The bin's XCTL_LIB default is /usr/lib/p5, which is exactly this directory.
file|client|/usr/lib/p5/xctl-lock.sh       |E5 |reserved|reconciler library: mkrun/mkfacts/take_lock (the single lock every lifecycle entry point funnels through)
file|client|/usr/lib/p5/xctl-probe.sh      |E5 |reserved|reconciler library: the reality-faithful probe registry (live_*/gl_*/wans/mode_of/svc_*/AGG_SCHED_TABLE/susp_state/node/desired)
file|client|/usr/lib/p5/xctl-actions.sh    |E5 |reserved|reconciler library: the leaf ACTIONS with real side effects (config builders, feeder start/stop, endpoint, MTU, revert)
file|client|/usr/lib/p5/xctl-shape.sh      |E5 |reserved|reconciler library: E4 shaping probe + leaves (INV8 -- contributes no guard and no verify)
file|client|/usr/lib/p5/xctl-dag.sh        |E5 |reserved|reconciler library: guards, verifies, the leaf dispatchers and the DAG interpreter (converge/converged/reconcile/selfcheck)
glob|both  |/var/run/p5/*                  |E5 |runtime |per-run volatile state (today /var/run/bond/*, e.g. the watchdog tput sample). The dir row above exists; without this glob the P5-half clean predicate cannot see a leftover inside it. It also admits the portal's job lock /var/run/p5/portal.job/ (U228: a lock is a statement about a RUNNING process, so it belongs on tmpfs where a reboot clears it -- the same reasoning xctl-lock.sh applies to the reconciler's own lock) and the datapath stats file /var/run/p5/datapath.stats (U225); neither gets a row of its own

# ---- E4 shaping: the controller and its payload root (U210) ----------------
# THE NAME IS FORCED, NOT CHOSEN. P5's shaper controller used to be spelled
# /etc/init.d/cake-autorate, which is P1's live service and a `p1|` row in
# contract/foreign -- p5_check_dest consults foreign BEFORE the namespace, so a
# filemap row at that destination is refused with exit 4 and no widening of the
# namespace could ever admit it (widening reddens NS-3). U210 renamed the
# controller into P5's namespace instead; these rows are a FILL of
# namespace:/etc/init.d/p5-* and /usr/lib/p5/*, not a widening.
# SHAPE_BASE is /usr/lib/p5/p5-shape and not /usr/lib/p5/cake-autorate for a
# second mechanical reason: the runtime-dir removal arm anchors on the basename
# set p5|p5-*|p5.*, so a directory named cake-autorate could not be removed by it.
file|client|/etc/init.d/p5-shape           |E4 |reserved|P5's shaper controller; placed DISABLED by p5-install from the filemap row, activated only by act_shape_apply, and driven only while it carries the P5 ownership marker (INV8)
dir |client|/usr/lib/p5/p5-shape           |E4 |runtime |SHAPE_BASE: current, config.p5.sh, v-<setid>/<STAGE names>. Created by p5-shape-install ON THE BOX, never by the installer (state=runtime, so a package that tried to ship into it is refused exit 4); removed by the MEMBER/RMDIR arms
glob|client|/usr/lib/p5/p5-shape/*         |E4 |runtime |so the P5-half clean predicate sees a leftover inside it and the removal arms may unlink it -- the same reason as the /var/run/p5/* row above

# ---- created AFTER install, by procd, when a service is enabled ------------
# `/etc/init.d/<svc> enable` writes /etc/rc.d/S<pri><svc> and the matching
# K<pri> symlink. The installer cannot record these: it enables nothing, and
# they do not exist while it runs. Declared as ONE glob rather than four rows
# because the priority digits are E5's derivation (same reason as the hotplug
# hook) and because the service SET is E5's to extend.
glob|both  |/etc/rc.d/[SK][0-9][0-9]p5-*    |E5 |enable  |procd start/stop flags for the P5 services. Created by `enable`, removed by `disable` -- removal calls `stop` then `disable` BEFORE it unlinks the init script, or the flag outlives its target and points at nothing. The P5-half clean predicate probes this glob, so a stale flag is visible

# ---- facts written on the box (E6's stateless fact-writer) -----------------
# Enumerated as a glob, not per-file: the fact SET is E6's to define, and
# guessing filenames here would be inventing the fact model.
#
# state=runtime, NOT reserved, and that is a real gate and not bookkeeping:
# E6's fact-writer runs ON THE BOX, so the installer must never be able to
# place a file under /etc/p5. An installer that seeds configuration is an
# installer that ships a constant -- the /etc/bond/agg_w 20000,15000 defect
# (removed in 52a76d3) is exactly this shape. Bar IN-16 packages a file into
# /etc/p5 and asserts exit 4.
glob|both  |/etc/p5/*                       |E6 |runtime |discovered facts (WG identity, the N sources, spotty-class). Names are E6's. Written on the box, never by the installer

# ---- named UCI objects P5 owns inside files it does NOT own ----------------
# See contract/foreign for the full argument. Short form: the unit of ownership
# for a UCI config file is the named OBJECT, not the file. /etc/config/firewall
# is GL native and stays on the foreign list, so nothing may ever INSTALL a
# file there; but the server cannot receive on the bonded UDP port unless
# something opens it, and the old stack's answer -- one named rule object,
# p2-engarde/bootstrap-bond-server.sh:57-63 `uci set firewall.engarde...` --
# is removable by name, which is exactly why the handover's removal list names
# the object (`its firewall rule (firewall.engarde)`,
# p5-execution-handover.md:80) and not the file. P5 takes the same shape under
# its own name. The port itself is NOT here and is not E0's: it is a value the
# owning unit must carry, not a constant this file invents.
#
# THIS ROW IS THE ONE PLACE P5 TOUCHES THE MANAGEMENT PATH, and it is why
# p5-deadman exists. Creating or deleting a firewall object and committing it
# reloads the firewall; on the server, the operator's own SSH session rides
# that firewall. E5 (create) and E7 (delete) MUST arm a deadman first. E0
# neither creates nor deletes it, which is asserted by bar MG-1.
uci |server|firewall.p5                     |E5 |uci     |the one named firewall rule that admits the bonded UDP port on the server. Created by E5's server bootstrap with `uci set firewall.p5...; uci commit firewall`, removed with `uci delete firewall.p5; uci commit firewall`. NOTHING writes it yet. P5 owns this OBJECT and no other object in that file

# ---- package staging (transient, NOT part of the removal contract) ---------
# tmpfs, cleared by reboot. The installer cleans it on success. Listed so that
# a reader looking for "where does the package land" finds the answer here and
# does not conclude removal must handle it.
staging|both|/tmp/p5-pkg                    |E8 |reserved|unpacked package staging dir; tmpfs, not persistent, not removed

# ---- the on-box validators (E8, U211; U176's decision (a) taken) -----------
# The three tools that decide whether an install is good. Until these rows
# existed they were shipped by nobody and declared un-shipped by nobody, so the
# only route onto a box was scp by hand (U176) -- the exact path the package
# exists to replace.
#
# A FILL, NOT A WIDENING. contract/namespace's `both|/usr/sbin/p5-*` row already
# admits all three names, so nothing here extends what P5 may own and NS-2..NS-5
# are unmoved. U176's row claimed the opposite ("adding an install destination
# widens the namespace"), and that false premise is what kept the gap open for
# three units.
#
# state=reserved, like every other filemap destination in this file: the
# installer places them FROM THE PACKAGE, so they must not enter the `install`
# set p5-install cross-checks against its own synthesised file list (IN-14 and
# IN-15 assert set equality in both directions over state=install rows only,
# p5_installable_paths in p5/lib/p5-common.sh). They ARE recorded in
# installed.files like every placed file, so `--remove` takes them away and the
# P5-half clean predicate sees a leftover.
#
# TWO PREFLIGHT ROWS AND NOT ONE DESTINATION WITH TWO ROLES: the package
# builder's duplicate check keys on the DEST column alone ("two filemap rows
# claim the same destination", scripts/build-p5-package.sh), so one dest
# carrying a client row and a server row would abort the build. Two names, one
# per box.
#
# APPENDED AT THE END OF THIS FILE ON PURPOSE. Inserting a block mid-file moves
# every line below it and rots every `contract/paths:N` citation in the repo --
# the U50a defect class. Order carries no meaning to any reader of this file
# (p5_check_dest scans it), so the cheap answer is the right one.
file|client|/usr/sbin/p5-accept            |E8 |reserved|P5's own acceptance runner (F23), repo file deploy/p5/bond-accept. Gate (4) of docs/knowledge/design/p5-orchestration.md, run on the box immediately before the deploy "go" -- and it must still run after /tmp and the unpacked package are gone, which is why it is INSTALLED rather than run from the package
file|client|/usr/sbin/p5-client-preflight  |E8 |reserved|the client's read-only preflight (U115), repo file deploy/p5/p5-client-preflight.sh. It REFUSES when /etc/p5/transport.key is absent, so it is a POST-install PRE-switch check and cannot usefully run from the package before the key exists
file|server|/usr/sbin/p5-server-preflight  |E8 |reserved|the server's read-only preflight (U38/U115), repo file deploy/server/p5-server-preflight.sh. Also runnable straight from the unpacked package before the install (`sh $PKG/payload/deploy/server/p5-server-preflight.sh`) for gate C1 of docs/deploy-p5-server.md; the installed copy is what an operator re-runs later on a box with no package under /tmp

# ---- the M9 portal: its own uhttpd instance, its own docroot (F17, U217) ----
# Mo decided 2026-09-05 that the portal is built before deployment, which is the
# one decision docs/FEATURES.md F17 named: a SECOND uhttpd listener on the
# client. These rows are the destination half of that decision. The portal
# itself is unchanged by them -- not one line of cgi/, lib/, www/, catalogue/ or
# init.d/p5-portal moved to make them fit.
#
# A FILL, NOT A WIDENING, exactly like the validator rows above:
# contract/namespace already admits `both|/usr/lib/p5/*` and
# `both|/etc/init.d/p5-*`, so nothing here extends what P5 may own and
# NS-2..NS-5 do not move.
#
# WHY THE CGI LANDS UNDER www/cgi-bin AND NOT UNDER cgi/. The init script starts
# uhttpd `-h $DOCROOT -x /cgi-bin` with DOCROOT=/usr/lib/p5/portal/www
# (deploy/p5/portal/init.d/p5-portal:30,74), and the page fetches the relative
